# What it abstracts
Hides what the system prompt is actually made of: the core knows no contributor by name, it knows only this contract.

# Description
Before every call to the model the system prompt is rebuilt from scratch — not read from a single file, but assembled from every participant's note. A contributor is exactly such a participant: it has a name and one method — "lay out everything you're adding right now."

Who actually writes these notes:

- the working mode — what the agent is doing right now: a one-off command-line run or a live chat;
- every agent feature brings its own piece of prompt and its own tools — file work adds instructions on how to read and edit files;
- every installed plugin adds its own text and a list of what it can do;
- if a skill is attached to the turn, the skills contributor inserts its text and its resources;
- working-memory keys prefixed context: enter the prompt on their own, without the model asking;
- tool-set registries report what is unfolded to the model now and what is waiting for a request;
- the project adds its own mount points and its own instructions, when they are declared.

Every note carries its author on record — so the question "who put this paragraph in the prompt" has an answer, and the prompt preview shows a human exactly what will ship to the model.

# Moment
Context assembly, before every call to the model — not once per turn. So a skill attached on the previous step, or a memory key written then, is visible to the model already on the next one.

# If bypassed
Gluing a string together somewhere off to the side breaks two things at once: the prompt preview (what is shown diverges from what is sent) and token accounting. A contributor is also not allowed to fail silently: one that falls over disappears from the prompt without a trace, so the composer catches its error and records it.

# What it abstracts
Hides the whole path from "the model named a tool" to "the tool started working": the tool itself knows nothing about this path.

# Description
When the model asks for a tool, the call does not go straight to the tool. It passes a queue of stages, and each is its own small inspector:

- find the tool by name and check its arguments against the schema;
- confirm the plugin the tool belongs to is currently available;
- compute which trust zones the call moves between (in shadow mode for now: it counts, it does not forbid);
- ask permission — of the policy, and if that does not know, of a human;
- give the call a node in the progress tree, so it is visible while it runs;
- send it to the background if the tool is long, and return the turn a receipt instead of a result;
- catch an exception and turn it into a clear refusal, not a crash of the turn.

A stage must be indifferent to what it wraps. A stage that knows a specific tool by name is a tool that moved to the wrong place.

# Moment
Between the model's answer and real work, on every call named in that answer.

# If bypassed
The order of the stages is itself the security policy: resolution before permissions, permissions before execution. Reordering them silently changes the threat model, breaking nothing that shows on the surface.
